<BaseLayout title="Acupressure Techniques: Learn Proper Methods" description="Learn proper acupressure techniques. Step-by-step guides for self-treatment, pressure methods, and daily routines." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Techniques", href: "/techniques/" }]}>
<div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
<h1 class="mb-4">Techniques</h1>
<p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-10">Master the proper techniques for effective acupressure self-treatment. Learn how to locate points, apply pressure, and build a daily practice.</p>
<section class="mb-8">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What You'll Find in This Section</h2>
<p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">Technique matters more than pressure strength in acupressure. The guide in this section walks you through locating points accurately, applying the right amount of pressure, and building a sustainable daily routine, all of which are the essentials for getting reliable results from self-treatment at home.</p>
<p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">The core method is simple to describe and takes practice to master: find the point using anatomical landmarks, confirm it by tenderness and a slight depression, press straight down at a 90-degree angle, and hold steady without rubbing or pulsing. The target sensation is a deep, dull ache called deqi, which signals that the point has been activated correctly. Sharp or shooting pain means you are pressing too hard.</p>
<p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Most points respond best to 30 seconds to 3 minutes of sustained pressure, with about 90 seconds per point a reliable baseline. A full session typically runs 10-20 minutes and covers 4-6 points, and you do not need to treat every point on the body in each session.</p>
</section>
<section class="mb-8">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Getting Started</h2>
<p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">Before you begin your first session, run through these fundamentals:</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-3">
<li><strong>Position yourself comfortably.</strong> Sit or lie down so the target area is fully relaxed. Tension in the surrounding muscles reduces the effectiveness of the pressure.</li>
<li><strong>Warm your hands first.</strong> Cold hands cause reflexive muscle tension, so rub your hands together or run them under warm water before starting.</li>
<li><strong>Build pressure gradually.</strong> Increase pressure over the first 10-15 seconds, aiming for about a 6 or 7 on a 1-to-10 scale, until you reach the deqi sensation.</li>
<li><strong>Hold steady and breathe.</strong> Maintain consistent pressure without massaging, and breathe deeply, which activates the parasympathetic nervous system and amplifies the effect.</li>
<li><strong>Release slowly and repeat.</strong> Remove pressure gradually over 3-5 seconds, then repeat on the opposite side, since most points are bilateral.</li>
</ul>
<p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">The full step-by-step guide below covers everything from what you need and how to find points to common mistakes and tips for best results.</p>
</section>
<section class="mb-8">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="space-y-4">
<details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
<summary class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 cursor-pointer">How much pressure should I use?</summary>
<p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Enough to feel a deep, dull ache, but never sharp pain. On a 1-to-10 scale, aim for 6 or 7; at that level you still breathe comfortably and relax into the sensation. If the feeling becomes sharp, ease off.</p>
</details>
<details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
<summary class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 cursor-pointer">How long should I hold each point?</summary>
<p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Standard clinical practice is 30 seconds to 3 minutes per point. Facial and sensitive areas need less; deeper points on larger muscles can take up to 3 minutes. Many people use 90 seconds as a reliable baseline.</p>
</details>
<details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
<summary class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 cursor-pointer">How often should I do acupressure?</summary>
<p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">For acute symptoms like a sudden headache or nausea, every 30-60 minutes as needed. For chronic conditions such as back pain or anxiety, once or twice daily. For general wellness, three to four times per week is usually enough.</p>
</details>
<details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
<summary class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 cursor-pointer">Can I press too hard?</summary>
<p class="mt-2 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Yes. More pressure is not better. Sharp pain causes the muscles to tighten defensively, which blocks the therapeutic effect. You want a deep, dull ache, not pain.</p>
</details>
</div>
</section>
